# Cutlass

Open-source, AI-first video editor, inspired by CapCut. Users describe an edit,
an agent turns it into structured commands, and Rust applies those commands to
the project. Edits are deterministic and undoable.

Cutlass is being rebuilt from scratch. Codecs come first.

## Stack

- Rust everywhere. One engine for desktop (macOS, Windows, Linux) and mobile
  (iOS, Android).
- Slint for the desktop UI. wgpu for preview, compositing, and export.

## Media policy

- No FFmpeg, GStreamer, or C codec libraries in the build. `deny.toml` bans the
  FFmpeg crates.
- Decode and encode with the OS codec first (`crates/codecs/native/`). Use the
  Rust codec (`crates/codecs/software/`) when the device has none.
- Export H.264 and AAC everywhere. HEVC only on a hardware encoder, otherwise
  H.264. ProRes encode on Apple silicon only.
- `docs/codecs.md` is the source of truth for codecs, platforms, and export.
  Change it before adding or dropping a codec.
- `docs/project.md` is the source of truth for the project, the timeline, a
  track, and a segment. Change it before adding a field.

## Planned: Python bindings

A MoviePy-style Python package is a side feature. It is a separate crate,
`cutlass-py` (PyO3 and maturin), kept out of the default workspace build. It
wraps the engine with a small Pythonic API (`add_clip`, `split`, `export`,
`get_frame`) and never exposes raw command enums. It needs the Rust exporter
first.
